ntation | P1 |\n\nImplementation types: E002 `repo-onboarding`, E003 `license-validation`,\nE004 `konflux-onboarding`, E008 `docs-authoring`; E005-E007 general.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[E001 Legal clearance] --> E002[E002 Repo onboarding]\n    E001 --> E003[E003 License validation]\n    E001 --> E004[E004 Konflux onboarding]\n    E001 --> E005[E005 Operator enablement]\n    E002 --> E004\n    E002 --> E005\n    E003 --> E005\n    E005 --> E006[E006 DSP defaults]\n    E005 --> E007[E007 Dashboard defaults]\n    E002 --> E008[E008 Documentation]\n    E003 --> E008\n    E004 --> E008\n    E005 --> E008\n    E006 --> E008\n    E007 --> E008\n```\n\nNote: there is deliberately **no** edge between E004 (Konflux onboarding) and\nE005 (operator enablement) — they run in parallel; the coupling is enforced by\nE005's \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion, not a DAG dependency.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 | Legal clearance (HLR-1) blocks all shipping work; the repo targets the cleared candidate. Gate type: `rewrite` (fallback candidate restarts image productization). |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | The license scan target depends on which candidate is cleared. Gate type: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 | Explicit gate edge (transitive via E002): onboarding is redone if the fallback upstream is selected. Gate type: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 | Explicit gate edge (transitive via E002/E003): enablement is reimplemented against the replacement store. Gate type: `rewrite`. |\n| E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 7 | repo-onboarding and license-validation have independent inputs; parallel start. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 5 | Konflux onboarding needs the repo; always serial. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 6 | General implementation of the onboarded component needs the repo. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 9 | If license scanning fails, dependencies change and the approach is affected; serial. |\n| (none) E004 ↔ E005 | Rule 10 | Konflux onboarding is parallel to general implementation; \"build pipeline green\" is an AC on E005, never a DAG edge. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rules 12/13 | DSP connection defaults build against the bundled store capability delivered by the operator. |\n| E005 → E007 | Rules 12/13 | Dashboard model serving connection defaults build against the bundled store capability. |\n| E006 ∥ E007 | Rule 14 | Different repos/components, no shared artifacts; parallel. |\n| E002..E007 → E008 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by all Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. |\n\n## Conditional decomposition decision\n\nThe gating Investigation (E001) has two bounded outcomes: (A) MinIO AGPL-3.0\nclearance granted, (B) fallback to an Apache-2.0 alternative (e.g. SeaweedFS).\nBoth outcomes yield the **same epic structure** — the same onboarding chain,\noperator enablement, consumer defaults, and docs — only the target upstream of\nthe image productization work changes. Because no downstream epic is added or\nremoved by the outcome, no `-BRANCH-` files were created; instead E002-E005\ncarry `gated_by=E001` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` describing the\nrestart of image productization against the fallback candidate. E006-E008 are\nnot gated: they target \"the bundled store\" identically under either outcome.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epics |\n|-----|----------|-------------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Legal review of redistributing the candidate store (MinIO, AGPL-3.0), including derivative-work posture | E001 (decision), E003 (transitive dependency license evidence) |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Administrators can enable a supported, bundled S3-compatible store via the Platform Operator with productized images and upgrade lifecycle | E002, E003, E004, E005 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | DSP and model serving connection defaults target the bundled store without manual endpoint configuration | E006 (DSP), E007 (model serving via Dashboard) |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation covers enablement, sizing guidance, and the supported/unsupported boundary | E008 |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Component Mappings\n\n- The bundled object store (MinIO or fallback) is a **new component** — it has\n  no entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and no architecture context file.\n  Per the component-name constraint, its epics (E002-E004, and the enablement\n  epic E005) use the closest parent-level canonical component, **Platform\n  Operator**, which is the bundling and enablement vehicle. Logged here as a\n  non-obvious mapping.\n- New-component detection triggered Rule 20: full onboarding chain\n  (repo-onboarding + license-validation parallel → konflux-onboarding → general\n  implementation).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n   owner-team column on Affected Components, no Dependencies table, no\n   Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements.\n   Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs and Affected Components list.\n2. **Team assignments are inferred**: \"RHOAI Pipelines\" and \"RHOAI Dashboard\"\n   come from the architecture context owning-team fields; \"RHOAI Platform\n   Operator\" and \"RHOAI Documentation\" are assumed by the same naming\n   convention. Verify with the strategy author.\n3. **Ambiguous component for HLR-3**: the HLR names \"model serving\" connection\n   defaults, but the Affected Components list contains Dashboard (no serving\n   runtime component). The work was mapped to the Dashboard (data connection /\n   serving configuration surface); if the intent was Serving Runtimes or KServe\n   code changes, E007's component assignment should be corrected.\n4. **Priority note (not an inversion)**: E008 is P1 despite covering P2 HLR-4,\n   via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority:\n   Major → P1). It does not push priority upstream to E002-E007.\n5. **Scope watch**: HLR-4's \"sizing guidance\" implies performance\n   characterization of the bundled store that no epic explicitly delivers;\n   sizing content will need input from implementation results (captured as an\n   AC on E008 rather than a new epic, since it is documentation content, not\n   infrastructure).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable as a split. The external dependency here is a legal decision,\nnot an upstream delivery with uncertain timing: HLR-1 states the clearance\nblocks **all** shipping work, so no Tier 1 user-facing value can ship ahead of\nthe clearance. Risk-reduction and setup work that can proceed in parallel with\nthe legal review is limited because the candidate itself may change; the\nonboarding chain is therefore fully gated. Partial value exists only across\nthe P0/P1 boundary (operator enablement ships before consumer defaults), which\nis already reflected in the DAG.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":27132,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af06d9472b104ca0","agent_id":"acaf9d9f56a8e19b4","session_id":"73da6f79-aa1d-41ce-bbe1-bfcaf3dba97b","uuid":"02a36949-930d-4dfb-b5f6-d25dd9a5db7e","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:48:13.874Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9012"}
